cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use quant-control-bench in your research, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "quant-control-bench: How Few Bits Does a Closed-Loop Robot Controller Need?"
authors:
  - family-names: "Happynood"
    given-names: "Alex"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-07-25"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Happynood/quant-control-bench"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - quantization
  - reinforcement-learning
  - robotics
  - control
  - mujoco
  - sim-to-real
abstract: >
  quant-control-bench measures how post-training quantization degrades
  closed-loop robot control policies, on a 4GB consumer GPU. Unlike language
  models, where quantization error is applied once per token, a controller
  recycles its error through the plant at every timestep, so a bounded per-step
  action error can produce unbounded trajectory divergence. The benchmark
  reports a feedback amplification factor (relative return drop divided by
  relative open-loop action error), a divergence horizon, and a robustness
  frontier under external pushes, mass and friction shift, actuator delay and
  observation noise, all with bootstrap confidence intervals from real MuJoCo
  runs. It ships a precision recommender that returns the cheapest scheme
  meeting a target robustness level and browser latency budget.
